---
### Finding a Failure
[build] @(70, 165) {$note, w=1140px, size=29px} **WAT validation error:** check operand types, the stack, and the declared result.
...
[fetch] @(70, 285) {$note, w=1140px, size=29px} **Fetch failure or HTTP 404:** check the server, the URL, and the `.wasm` filename.
...
[export] @(70, 405) {$note, w=1140px, size=29px} **Missing function:** JavaScript uses the exact export string `CalcHypotenuse`.
...
[old] @(70, 525) {$note, w=1140px, size=29px} **Old output after a WAT edit:** rebuild the binary and reload the page.
[tools] @(70, 640) {$note, w=1140px, size=25px, color=emp} The browser's Console and Network tabs help identify which step failed.
---
### The Same Workflow in Project 3
[compiler] @(70, 165) {$note, w=1140px, size=30px} Your compiler generates WAT from the source program.
...
[runner] @(70, 285) {$note, w=1140px, size=30px} The test script runs your compiler, then uses `wat2wasm` to build the binaries.
...
[server] @(70, 415) {$note, w=1140px, size=30px} Serve the project's `tests/` directory and open its test page to run the Wasm.
...
[check] @(70, 555) {$note, w=1140px, size=29px, color=emp} A valid Wasm module can still compute the wrong answer. Test its results as well as its syntax.
% Source: web/project3.md, "Run the tests" and "Load the results".
